allup score  when we look at the rock curve  for gallaudet in comparison to  single tumor marker including alpha  fetal protein  protein iii and dcp we see  that the gallon outperform  each individual markers and those are  two series from  uk and japan  in a trial where they look at galat  following reception they had 326  patients with hcc undergoing reception  percent 195 patients recur  while 131 didn't when  after a section the gallup score had  declined in all patients however  the decline was most marked in patients  that  had no recurrence by three years which  led  to believe that maybe serial measurement  of gallup  may permit early detection of recurrence  how this data is going to be used  prospectively  is yet to be seen  another model is the moral model the  moral model is model of recurrence after  liver transplant  currently liver transplant follows say  the milan criteria are used to allocate  liver  to patient with hcc and it only depend  on the disease the size of the disease  and the patient characteristics they  don't  incorporate tumor biology  and this is what this model is trying to  do is looking at alpha fetal protein  at pivka in this simple calculation to  see if that  predict the recurrence free survival and  overall survival in patient undergoing  transplant  it is simple it's highly accurate tool  for predicting recurrence and risk  stratification pre  and post-operatively again further  studies are needed for this model as  well  for it to be validated for future use  the next model is the asap asap model or  sap  asap stands for age sex alpha  phetoprotein and pivka2  this is another model that includes  those four variable  it was tested recently in a multi-center  trial for patient with hcc  secondary to hbv in asia it's an  online free use calculator  based on the most recent study  sensitivity was as high as 76 percent  and specificity was 90 percent in  detecting  early stage hcc or detecting hcc  this is how the model look like so you  are  giving point based on sex age  alpha fetal protein and pivka2 and then  you calculate the total mine at total  points  and that total point will predict your  risk  for example a patient who is 28 year old  with alpha fetal protein of 3.1 and 5k  value of 19  his risk is very low at 4.3  percent or probability of hcc risk and  this number can be used to decide on  what type of further  uh workup or how often he needs more  imaging or more testing  obviously if looking here when the alpha  fetal protein is higher and the  value is higher we are at risk of 44  and in this case the current risk  of developing cc is high and the patient  would require immediate  further imaging including triple phase  ct or an mri  or further evaluation obviously if the  numbers  are much higher like in this panel and  the  estimated risk is or probability of 54  percent this patient need also  further imaging and evaluation because  he is at a higher risk  of already developing hcc  the two gallon versus asap in general  the variable overlapped in both they are  using  similar or similar or the same variable  other than alpha protein 3  it was not part of the asap model  because it was insignificant  in predicting hcc in multivariate model  multivariable model  that uh limited use in hepatitis b in  particular  gallup has not been yet validated in  patient with hpv  there was a retrospective comparison  that showed that asap  normogram be better predictive of hcc  however everything has limitation these  are all small study  there is a lot of selection bias giving  the design of the study  patient with hcc even with the asap were  diagnosed at more  advanced stage some patients who did not  have hcc still had  elevated alpha phytoprotein and 5k2  leading to a high  risk calculation longer follow-up and  specific study  in patients with elevated pivka3 are  still needed  and of course anything need external  validation especially studies that  compare  that test to the standard of care  which is currently ultrasound and alpha  fetal protein these study are also  needed  again the data based on the most current  study are based on a single assay  and it was for patient with hbv so  validation in non-hpv patients is still  needed  in conclusion hcc is a common and  increasing malignancy  it is rapidly rising cause of liver  related death in the united states and  in the world  screening and surveillance for liver  cancer could save life  with early detection where patients have  several treatment and putative options  multiple societies still recommend hcc  screening  in high-risk patients  with all the data i presented i think  it's important to understand the  strength  the weakness and the limitation of the  available screening methods  is important all these new combination  of tests and normogram  are showing promising results however it  need to be validated more in future  prospective studies  and integrating this biomarker in hcc  treatment pathway as a surrogate for  treatment response  or disease recurrence which is important  to us as oncologists  or to surgeon is extremely important  this will end my presentation thank you  very much and i'll be happy to take any  questions  thank you dr amhanna for that  outstanding presentation  we will now move into the live q a  portion of the presentation  as a reminder please submit your  questions via the q a box  our first question is do you see these  models or markers  incorporated in transplant criteria and  organs allocations  as i said the current transplant  criteria include  patients and tumor characteristics so  it's tumor size  patients characteristic including  alcohol use  uh compliance psych issues and  infections  it would be nice to incorporate these  criteria but  it needs to be really validated  prospectively before  it is used as  a criteria for transplant or allocation  of organs  now are any of these tests currently  being evaluated in patients following  liver transplant  surgical resection as a predictive for  disease recurrence  so i mentioned that the galad model has  been  used or looked at as a predictive of  recurrence following  resection and following uh transplant  however  really how that affects uh  treatment algorithm or frequency of  imaging  is yet to be determined because if we're  going to depend only on the numbers  we're going to  order more tests to increase incl cause  more anxiety  and unnecessary testing  without really changing the outcome so  again this need to be validated  prospectively before  we incorporate it and of course it has  to  we have to make sure that it will  affect the overall outcome and survival  now dr alhana with the chambers in the  landscape of treating metastatic hcc  do you see a role of using these models  in treatment selections or  monitoring response  so currently all the drugs that are  approved for hcc  don't have any predictive marker for  response  uh tumor markers especially a fetal  protein  is used as a surrogate for response to  treatment  however numbers are numbers and they  should really be used as surrogate not  as  only predictive of response  we still are going to depend on scanning  and imaging and clinical pictures  however if we have something that's very  sensitive and specific  it will sure be helping guiding uh  following response to treatment not  choosing the treatment  and it looks like we have time for one  more question is there any role  of incorporating cdna in any of these  models  so circulating dna is currently being  used or tested in several malignancy it  has gone further in certain magnets than  others  in hcc it has not been established as a  predictive or a prognostic model  uh and it hasn't been part of the  standard of care obviously  we would like to have more data and  incorporating circulating dna  with these markers to predict uh  response  or risk of recurrence and again these  are all  very immature and still early in  development  thank you again dr omaha and thank you  to the audience for your outstanding  questions  we hope you found today's presentation  to be informative and insightful  this presentation will be available for  on-demand viewing  don't miss out on the other valuable  presentations on our agenda visit the  agenda tab in the auditorium full  for a full listing thank you again for  your participation  and until next time goodbye
 

